PG0 found that no dollar of revenue in this database can be traced to the
tool that produced it. This is the patch that ends that, and it is the
first item the transformation brief lists under Program 1: DESIGN IDENTITY
→ Design ID.

WHY THE FIELD WE ALREADY HAD COULD NOT DO IT

`encodeStudioDesign` already travels from the studio to /api/leads, and it
looks like an identifier. It is not one. It is DETERMINISTIC. Two strangers
who both choose white oak, satin, straight, 5", 900 sq ft produce a
byte-identical string, and `studioRef` hashes that string, so they also
print the same FS- reference. Joining leads on it would silently merge
unrelated people into one "design" — and merge them harder the better a
floor sells, so the most popular floor would be the most wrongly counted.

That is not an assertion in a commit message. It is the first test in
design-id.test.ts, which builds two designs from different sessions and
asserts the codes are equal. If that test ever fails, this patch was
unnecessary.

WHAT WAS ADDED

lib/floor-studio/design-id.ts — 12 characters of Crockford base32 (no i,
l, o or u, so a reference read down a phone line cannot become a different
one), minted from crypto.getRandomValues. It is random rather than derived
from the design (which collides) and rather than derived from the device
(which would be a fingerprint — a different product, built by accident).
It carries no timestamp. It authorises nothing.

It names a DESIGN, not a person. A link handed to a spouse, a designer or
a realtor opens the same id, which is the feature Program 1 asks for under
design collaboration. The cost is stated in the module and in the test: it
counts designs that converted, not people — where those differ it
undercounts sessions, never invents them.

THE CHAIN, END TO END

  studio mints → share code `d=` → estimateHref `did=` → hidden form field
  → leadSchema → QuoteRequest.designId → Project.designId

Both columns are VarChar(16), nullable, indexed, added by an additive
migration that is safe to deploy against a live database. The index is the
point: without it the attribution query is a sequential scan over every
quote request the business has ever taken, which is the kind of query that
gets written once, runs slowly, and is never run again.

`convertQuoteToProject` copies the id forward in one line, so "of the last
100 deposits, how many began in Floor Studio" is answerable without walking
Payment → Invoice → Project → QuoteRequest.

`did` is read from the querystring INDEPENDENTLY of `design`. If a share
code fails validation — a retired floor, a truncated link — the
configuration is correctly discarded, but the fact that this person came
from a studio design is still true. Losing the origin along with the
configuration would make attribution worse exactly where the funnel
already leaks.

GA4 carries `design_id` on studio_visualised, studio_share,
studio_estimate_handoff, studio_samples_request and quote_submit — the
denominator for the conversion the database supplies the numerator of.
`track()` now accepts undefined and STRIPS it rather than sending it: a
parameter that is always empty registers in GA4 as a real dimension, and
somebody eventually builds a report on it.

THREE THINGS THAT WOULD HAVE GONE WRONG QUIETLY

1. The id is excluded from studioRef's hash input. Two people on one share
   link must read the same reference aloud; putting a random value into
   that hash would have renamed an unchanged floor on every mint.

2. The id is minted in an effect and set BACK INTO REACT STATE, not left
   inside saveStudioDesign. Minted only inside the helper it would have
   reached localStorage and nothing else — not the URL, not the share link,
   not the handoff. That is precisely the half-wired shape PG0 found
   everywhere else on this site.

3. decodeStudioDesign SPREADS the key rather than writing
   `designId: undefined`, so a pre-MEAS-01 link round-trips byte-identical.
   vitest's toEqual would have hidden that; two existing assertions in
   studio-config.test.ts did not, and they are why this is correct.

PRIVACY

Unchanged, deliberately. Floor Studio still analyses photos in the browser
and retains none. The id never touches an image. The brief calls that
stance a strategic advantage; an identifier that reached a photo would
spend it.

Verified: 65 of 66 guards pass here (verify:css needs postcss from
node_modules, which this sandbox does not have — it is re-run in full
before the push). Migrations guard clean: 26 models, 17 enums, 8
migrations, no drift. parse-scan 603 files, zero diagnostics — it caught an
import this patch had inserted into the middle of a multi-line import
block, which is the second time that check has paid for itself. 70 new
assertions; 68,084 across the local suite.

